<section
  id='case-studiobid-logotype'
  class='fullscreen-section case-study-section'
  data-title='Case Study: StudioBid — Brand &amp; Logotype | Katja Turnsek'
  data-description='Graphic design case study for StudioBid, focused on a recognisable auction identity and flexible logo system designed for digital product use.'
  data-case='studiobid'
  aria-labelledby='case-studiobid-logotype-title'
>
  <div class='rows'>
    <div class='row'>
      <div class='case-study-header'>
        <p class='case-eyebrow'>Graphic Design</p>
        <h1 id='case-studiobid-logotype-title'>StudioBid</h1>
        <p class='case-subtitle'>
          A direct auction identity designed to remain clear and recognisable throughout the digital
          product.
        </p>

        <dl class='case-summary' aria-label='Project summary'>
          <div>
            <dt>Role</dt>
            <dd>Brand identity and logo design</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Scope</dt>
            <dd>Individual Semester Project 2</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Year</dt>
            <dd>2025</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Tools</dt>
            <dd>Adobe Illustrator, Figma</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Outcome</dt>
            <dd>UI-ready logo system</dd>
          </div>
        </dl>

        <div class='tech-demo-wrapper case-demo-wrapper'>
          <div class='demo-links' data-auto='demo' data-case='studiobid-logotype'></div>
        </div>

        <nav
          class='project-switcher'
          aria-label='StudioBid sections'
          data-auto='switcher'
          data-case='studiobid'
        ></nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class='row'>
      <h2 class='case-section-heading'>The brief</h2>
      <p>
        StudioBid needed an identity that communicated its purpose as an online auction platform
        without requiring an explanation. The logo had to fit naturally into the interface, remain
        recognisable as a small icon, and work across both light and dark backgrounds. It also
        needed enough personality to distinguish the platform from more traditional auction brands.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class='row'>
      <div class='case-study-grid'>
        <article class='case-study-block case-study-block--challenge'>
          <h2>Challenge</h2>
          <p>
            A gavel is an obvious symbol for an auction, which makes it useful but also very
            familiar. The main challenge was giving this symbol enough character to feel connected
            to StudioBid while keeping it simple enough for navigation, favicons, and other small
            interface placements.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>Make the platform's purpose immediately recognisable</li>
            <li>
              Give a familiar auction symbol its own colour and visual character
            </li>
            <li>
              Create variations for narrow, small, light, and dark placements
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class='case-study-item case-study-media--explore'>
          <img
            class='thumb'
            src='{{base}}assets/images/design-studiobid-logosuite-600.webp'
            data-src='design-studiobid-logosuite-600.webp'
            data-path='assets/images'
            width='600'
            height='391'
            loading='lazy'
            decoding='async'
            alt='StudioBid logo suite showing primary and secondary wordmarks, gavel icons, monochrome versions, and reversed applications'
          />
          <figcaption class='case-caption'>
            Logo variations created for navigation, icons, and contrasting backgrounds
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <article class='case-study-block case-study-block--decisions'>
          <h2>Key decisions</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>
              I reduced the gavel to a bold silhouette so it remains easy to recognise when used
              alone at icon and favicon sizes.
            </li>
            <li>
              I highlighted “Bid” in vivid pink to draw attention to the platform's main action and
              give the wordmark a memorable point of contrast.
            </li>
            <li>
              The heavy geometric lettering balances the symbol and gives the identity a confident,
              contemporary character.
            </li>
            <li>
              I prepared primary, secondary, icon-only, monochrome, and reversed versions, then
              tested them inside the actual interface rather than only presenting them as separate
              graphics.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class='case-study-item case-study-media--booking'>
          <img
            class='thumb'
            src='{{base}}assets/images/design-studiobid-600.webp'
            data-src='design-studiobid-600.webp'
            data-path='assets/images'
            width='600'
            height='434'
            loading='lazy'
            decoding='async'
            alt='StudioBid gavel icon and wordmark displayed at large size on a white background'
          />
          <figcaption class='case-caption'>
            Primary mark combining the simplified gavel with the action-led wordmark
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class='row case-outcome'>
      <h2 class='case-section-heading'>Outcome and reflection</h2>
      <p>
        The finished identity is intentionally direct. The gavel communicates the auction concept
        quickly, while the pink accent and compact logo variations give StudioBid a consistent
        presence across the product. Designing the identity alongside the interface helped me treat
        the logo as a working part of the user experience rather than only as a graphic for
        presentation.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class='back-to-work-container'>
      <a
        href='{{base}}#work'
        class='label-link back-to-work-label'
        data-back
        data-back-fallback='{{base}}#work'
        aria-label='Back to Work'
      >
        <svg class='icon icon-18 arrow-icon' aria-hidden='true'>
          <use href='#i-arrow-left' />
        </svg>
        <span class='label-text'>Back to Work</span>
      </a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>